[0m[38;2;255;255;255m⏺[0m A network has no central controller to tell senders how fast to go, so each sender must find out for itself. The
  problem became plain in October 1986, when throughput on a link between two Berkeley sites fell from 32 kbit/s to 40
  bit/s because senders answered lost packets by sending more. Van Jacobson published the remedy in 1988, and its core
  idea still holds: probe for capacity, and back off at the first sign of trouble.

  The sender keeps a [0m[1mcongestion window[0m, called [0m[38;2;177;185;249mcwnd[0m, which caps the bytes it may have in flight without
  acknowledgement. An acknowledgement says that data got through. A lost packet says that a queue somewhere overflowed.
  The classic scheme, [0m[38;2;177;185;249mReno[0m, moves through three phases:

  1. [0m[1mSlow start[0m: the window begins at a few segments and grows by one segment per acknowledgement, so it doubles every
     round trip, until it hits a threshold called [0m[38;2;177;185;249mssthresh [0mor a loss.
  2. [0m[1mCongestion avoidance[0m: above the threshold the window grows by about one segment per round trip, a gentle linear
     probe for spare capacity.
  3. [0m[1mFast recovery[0m: three duplicate acknowledgements imply one lost segment, so the sender resends it and halves the
     window. A timeout is graver: the window falls to one segment and slow start begins again.

  The rules fit in a few lines of C:

